`preferredLayout` is computed from the mindmap-specific config but is never applied:
```typescript
data4Layout.layoutAlgorithm = getRegisteredLayoutAlgorithm(layout, { fallback: 'cose-bilkent' });
const preferredLayout = conf?.layoutAlgorithm ?? layout ?? 'cose-bilkent';
// preferredLayout is logged but never assigned to data4Layout.layoutAlgorithm
```
The mindmap config's `layoutAlgorithm` property is therefore always ignored at runtime. Either assign `data4Layout.layoutAlgorithm = getRegisteredLayoutAlgorithm(preferredLayout, { fallback: 'cose-bilkent' })` or remove the unused variable.

The collinearity check for `r1`/`r2` was semantically changed between the original and the new version:
```javascript
// Original (correct): non-zero same-sign r1, r2 means both points on same side
if (r1 !== 0 && r2 !== 0 && sameSign(r1, r2)) { return; }
// New: both near-zero AND same-sign — the opposite semantic
if (Math.abs(r1) < epsilon && Math.abs(r2) < epsilon && sameSign(r1, r2)) { return; }
```
The original check prevents intersection when both endpoints of segment p are on the same side of segment q. The new check only triggers when both values are *near zero* (i.e., both points nearly on line q), which is the collinear case — but that is already handled by the `denom === 0` check below. This change effectively removes the same-side guard, which could cause `intersectLine` to return spurious intersections for all diagrams that use this shared utility.

The `@ts-ignore` directive has only a TODO comment as justification:
```typescript
// @ts-ignore TODO fix this (KNSV)
return this.calcIntersect(node as Bounds, point);
```
Per project rules, `@ts-ignore` must explain *why* suppression is needed. More importantly, using `this` inside a regular `function` expression depends on call context — if `intersect` is extracted and called as a standalone function, `this` will not be `node`. Consider using a closure over `node` instead: `return node.calcIntersect(node as Bounds, point)`. Also, the `- 0.5` adjustment in `calcIntersect` is an unexplained magic number.
